From a528aeed4c2830217b26f5943b2b513a4117607b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 12:34:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] um: do not panic on shutdown when uml_dir was never created

remove_umid_dir() is an exitcall, so it runs on every shutdown whatever
happened at boot, and it calls strlen(uml_dir) without looking.
make_uml_dir() leaves uml_dir NULL whenever it could not create the
directory, so a kernel that failed to make its umid directory panics as
it halts:

	Kernel panic - not syncing: Kernel mode fault at addr 0x0
	 [<...>] do_uml_exitcalls
	 [<...>] machine_halt

with the backtrace pointing at the exitcall loop and nothing at the
directory that was never made.

This is the ordinary case rather than a corner: the default uml_dir is
under $HOME, and running the kernel straight from a shell with no
writable home is how anyone tries it the first time. It was found by
running the exact command line shipped with the prebuilt binaries, which
panicked on every clean shutdown.

Every other user of uml_dir is downstream of a make_umid() whose error it
already checks, so this is the only site that needs the test.

Signed-off-by: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
---
 arch/um/os-Linux/umid.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/um/os-Linux/umid.c b/arch/um/os-Linux/umid.c
index 705c09c86f1c1..f9f2bc32cdf3f 100644
--- a/arch/um/os-Linux/umid.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Linux/umid.c
@@ -413,6 +413,27 @@ static void remove_umid_dir(void)
 {
 	char *dir, err;
 
+	/*
+	 * make_uml_dir() leaves uml_dir NULL when it could not create the
+	 * directory, and this is an exitcall: it runs on every shutdown
+	 * whatever happened at boot. Without this check the strlen() below
+	 * faults at address zero and the kernel panics as it halts --
+	 *
+	 *	Kernel panic - not syncing: Kernel mode fault at addr 0x0
+	 *	 [<...>] do_uml_exitcalls
+	 *
+	 * -- with the backtrace pointing at the exitcall loop and nothing at
+	 * the directory that was never made. Every other user of uml_dir is
+	 * downstream of a make_umid() that has already returned an error, so
+	 * this is the only one that has to look.
+	 *
+	 * It is the ordinary case, not a corner: the default uml_dir sits
+	 * under $HOME, and a bare shell with no writable home is exactly how
+	 * someone runs this the first time.
+	 */
+	if (uml_dir == NULL)
+		return;
+
 	dir = malloc(strlen(uml_dir) + UMID_LEN + 1);
 	if (!dir)
 		return;
